  "worked_solution": "We need a vector $v=\u27e8{t}_{1},{t}_{2},{t}_{3}\u27e9$ that is parallel to the given plane. \n\nFrom the equation $r\\cdot \u27e86,-2,0\u27e9=-14$ of the plane, we get the normal vector $n=\u27e86,-2,0\u27e9.$ So, $v\u22a5n$ and we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ \u27e8{t}_{1},{t}_{2},{t}_{3}\u27e9\\cdot \u27e86,-2,0\u27e9 & =0 \\\\ 6{t}_{1}-2{t}_{2} & =0 \\\\ {t}_{2} & =3{t}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we obtain \n\n$$\nv=\u27e8{t}_{1},3{t}_{1},{t}_{3}\u27e9,\n$$\n\nwhere ${t}_{1},{t}_{3}\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ \n\nWe are told that the third component of $v$ eqauls $0.$ So, we must set ${t}_{3}=0.$ \n\nSince $v$ is nonzero, we can pick any ${t}_{1}\\ne 0.$ For example, setting ${t}_{1}=1$, we get\n\n$$\nv=\u27e81,\\,3,\\,0\u27e9.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{1}{3}.$",
